 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-15",
  "asof": "2026-10-16 09:29 UTC",
  "headline": "AMZN stopped out in the gap \u2014 and the stock closed higher on the day",
  "standfirst": "Amazon gapped below its breakeven stop at the open and was taken out 95 cents under it, at 250.89 against a 251.84 line \u2014 realising \u22122.85, or \u22120.14R, rather than nothing \u2014 before falling to 248.65 and recovering six dollars to close 254.76, up 0.27% and above the stop. Behind it, the week's argument resolved: PPI came in at nearly three times forecast and retail sales rose 0.8% against 0.3% expected, but the control group managed only 0.1% and ex-petrol the gain was 0.2%, so the ten-year fell about 12 basis points to roughly 5.07% and the thirty-year to 5.41% \u2014 a fifth consecutive session of the long end easing, and 26 basis points below its 7 October level. Hike odds for 28 October fell back from about 50% to roughly 27%. The Russell rose 1.38% and the Dow 1.08% while the Nasdaq managed 0.08%; over the week the Dow is the only index up. WTI jumped 3.03% to $97.97 on a US inventory draw while Brent fell to $105.92, collapsing the spread to $7.95. In the book GOOG recovered to +0.37%, OKTA fell 1.60% after being down 4.2% at its low, and AAPL was the only holding that did not bounce. A net of -12.67. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged even after a position closed \u2014 the stop sat at the fill and carried no risk. The foldable opens for pre-order on Friday.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "the pre-order today \u00b7 the Fed on the 28th",
    "title": "The pre-order opens in a few hours, and it is the only checkable thing on the list",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open</b>, 5am Pacific \u2014 sell-through and shipping-date slippage are visible within hours, and BofA calls it the indicator of premium demand",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-16",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo on sale</b> in the US and 70-plus countries from <b>$1,999</b>, with 28 more countries on the 30th",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> and <b>Alphabet Q3</b> on the same day \u2014 hike odds went 22% to 50% to about 27% in three sessions",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL \u00b7 AMZN",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 and Amazon Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 the first iPhone 18 quarter against two contradictory supply-chain reports",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>October CPI</b> \u2014 with crude three dollars higher than when this week began",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-11-10",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>Friday is the first thing in three weeks that produces evidence rather than sourcing.</b> <b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open at 5am Pacific</b>, with sale on the <b>23rd</b> at <b>$1,999</b> for 256GB rising to $3,199 for 2TB, and 28 further countries on the 30th. Three parties now disagree about iPhone 18 demand and all three are working from supply-chain contacts: <b>Nikkei</b> says Pro production is being cut; <b>Securities Times</b> says Pro supply targets are unchanged and <b>Duo orders were raised 30%</b> toward 10 million units; <b>Bank of America</b>, Buy at <b>$370</b>, reports Pro delivery times shorter than a year ago. Pre-order sell-through and how fast shipping dates slip are checkable facts, and they arrive thirteen days before Apple has to explain the quarter."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>Friday is the first thing in three weeks that produces evidence rather than sourcing.</b> <b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open at 5am Pacific</b>, with sale on the <b>23rd</b> at <b>$1,999</b> for 256GB rising to $3,199 for 2TB, and 28 further countries on the 30th. Three parties now disagree about iPhone 18 demand and all three are working from supply-chain contacts: <b>Nikkei</b> says Pro production is being cut; <b>Securities Times</b> says Pro supply targets are unchanged and <b>Duo orders were raised 30%</b> toward 10 million units; <b>Bank of America</b>, Buy at <b>$370</b>, reports Pro delivery times shorter than a year ago. Pre-order sell-through and how fast shipping dates slip are checkable facts, and they arrive thirteen days before Apple has to explain the quarter.",
